        ]
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          Starting out seems easy. Gutenberg blocks are pretty well documented, they have a solid structure and all the necessary data embedded into them, right?
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <View
      style={
        {
          "borderRadius": 10,
          "overflow": "hidden",
          "width": "100%",
        }
      }
    >
      <Image
        source={
          {
            "uri": "https://placehold.net/400x400.png",
          }
        }
        style={
          [
            {
              "aspectRatio": 1.3333333333333333,
            },
            {
              "width": "100%",
            },
          ]
        }
      />
    </View>
    <Text
      style={
        {
          "fontStyle": "italic",
        }
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          Not everything is as simple as it seems.
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        [
          {},
          {},
        ]
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          Let's take a look at the structure first. Although it does seem well defined, Gutenberg is still being updated, and things constantly change. For example - a List block would have all its contents embedded into innerHTML and no ListItem blocks in early days.
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        {
          "backgroundColor": "#555",
          "borderRadius": 5,
          "padding": 10,
        }
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text
          style={
            {
              "fontFamily": "Menlo",
            }
          }
        >
          <Text>
            "blockName":"core\\/list",
"attrs":[],
"innerBlocks":[],
"innerHTML":"\\n
&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;
    &lt;a href=\\"https:\\/\\/google.com\\/"
       target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noreferrer
       noopener\\"&gt;
      google.com
    &lt;\\/a&gt;
  &lt;\\/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;second list element
  &lt;\\/li&gt;
&lt;\\/ul&gt;\\n",
"innerContent":[...]
          </Text>
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        [
          {},
          {},
        ]
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          As showcased in the provided snippet, all of the list contents are squished into innerHTML rather than placing each element in a separate block and then into innerBlocks. Nowadays it follows a proper structure, but it's still necessary to handle old versions for backwards compatibility.
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        [
          {},
          {},
        ]
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={{}}
      >
        

      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        {
          "fontSize": 24,
          "fontWeight": 700,
          "marginTop": 30,
        }
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          Stylish choices
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        [
          {},
          {},
        ]
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          Backwards compatibility is somewhat expected. But what about custom WordPress themes? Should we try to parse them? Maybe we can write an additional translator from WordPress theme to some json specification and then parse each element trying to apply the styles? While this seems like a valid choice it's once again way more complicated...
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        [
          {},
          {},
        ]
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          Custom themes do not only introduce styling. They can change block structure and do all sorts of things. Then there is a mobile app theme vs WordPress theme. Just imagine WordPress using light theme and your app using a dark one! That would be a total disaster!
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        [
          {},
          {},
        ]
      }
    >
      <Text
        style={
          [
            {},
            {},
          ]
        }
      >
        <Text>
          Instead, what we can do is simplify it. Instead of parsing every little tiny thing, we can only take into account what's important! Like layout! Combine this with a lookup table and a list of allowed tags to parse and voila! This way you can use React Native components with a WordPress like layout and have the best of both worlds!
        </Text>
      </Text>
    </Text>
  </View>
  <View
    style={
      {
        "marginVertical": 5,
      }
    }
  >
    <Text
      style={
        [
